    "result_text": "All tests pass and the build succeeds.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephone numbers in the PetClinic Spring Boot app.\n\n**Change** \u2014 `src/main/java/.../rest/validation/TelephoneNormalizer.java`:\n\n- Added a `NATIONAL_DIGITS_BY_COUNTRY_CODE` map declaring the required national-digit count for each recognized country code (`\"61\"` \u2192 9, `\"1\"` \u2192 10).\n- Added `hasValidNationalLength(digits)`, which finds the **longest matching** country-code prefix and requires the remaining (national) digits to be exactly that country's length. Unrecognized country codes fall through to the existing generic 8\u201315 digit bound, preserving prior behavior.\n- Wired the check into `toE164(...)` so a wrong national length returns `null`, which `normalize(...)` turns into an `InvalidTelephoneException` \u2014 already mapped to **400 Bad Request** by `ExceptionControllerAdvice`.\n- Updated the exception message and Javadoc to document the per-country rule.\n\n**How it satisfies the spec and tests:**\n- The new test `errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` posts `\"+61 123\"` \u2192 cleaned to `61123`, country code `61` matches, national `123` is 3 digits \u2260 9 \u2192 rejected with 400. \u2713\n- The base `uniqueTelephone()` (`04XXXXXXXX`) normalizes to `+61` with 9 national digits \u2192 still valid, so no regression in the acceptance chain.\n- Seed data (`+61`-defaulted 10-digit numbers) only feeds the duplicate-check via `toE164`, where a `null` simply means \"no duplicate match\" \u2014 harmless.\n\n`BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.",
